# Review ownership for Aperture.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO VERIFY THIS FILE ACTUALLY DOES ANYTHING — read before editing.
#
# A CODEOWNERS file fails SILENTLY. An owner that cannot be resolved is not an error, not a
# warning, and not a failed check: the rule simply assigns nobody, and the file goes on
# looking like governance while enforcing nothing. So the owner must be verified, not
# assumed. Two rules follow from that:
#
#   1. An owner must be an INDIVIDUAL ACCOUNT (`@name`) or a TEAM (`@org/team-name`).
#      A bare organisation handle (`@org`) is NOT a valid owner on GitHub and is never
#      assigned. If you are tempted to write one because it feels more neutral than naming
#      an account, do not — that is the broken choice, not the conservative one.
#   2. Never guess a team name to avoid naming an account. A team that does not exist fails
#      exactly as silently as a bare org handle.
#
# To verify an owner on the forge where reviews happen, put the account on a branch
# protection push whitelist through the sanctioned forge tool. The forge validates the
# username and rejects an unknown one outright ("user does not exist", HTTP 422), so
# acceptance is positive proof the account resolves. That is how `@moose` below was
# verified: the whitelist call was accepted for it, and refused for a control name that
# does not exist.
#
# STATUS ON THE PUBLIC MIRROR — an operator action is outstanding.
# The mirror is a read-only, PII-swept derivative. No pull request is opened, reviewed, or
# merged there, so CODEOWNERS is inert on the mirror BY DESIGN and its inertness is not a
# defect. But it also means the owner below has NOT been confirmed to resolve on the public
# host, and it cannot be confirmed from inside this repository. If public contribution is
# ever opened, an operator must first create or confirm a real reviewing team there
# (`@org/team-name` form) and update this file. Until then, treat the mirror copy of this
# file as documentation of intent, not as an active review rule.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HONESTY NOTE: this project has exactly ONE maintainer identity. There is no review team,
# no per-area owner group, and no rotation — inventing one here would misrepresent who is
# actually reviewing, and would silently break the file besides. Every rule below therefore
# resolves to the same owner. The file exists so ownership is explicit, and so per-area
# owners can later be added by editing rules rather than by introducing the concept.
#
# `@moose` is a forge ROLE ACCOUNT — the maintainer and merge-queue identity. It is not a
# person's name, and no personal name or email address appears in this file or may be added
# to it, because this file ships publicly.
#
# Rules are last-match-wins: the global rule first, specific paths after it.
#
# ONLY PATTERNS THAT MATCH SOMETHING. A pattern matching no real path silently disables
# review for what its author thought it covered, so a stale rule is worse than a missing
# one. Patterns here are checked against the tree. Directories that do not exist yet — the
# client workspace, the contract tree, the end-to-end suite, CI configuration — are covered
# by the global rule below and get their own rules when they land, not before.

# Everything, including any path added in future.
*                        @moose

# Governance and pipeline configuration. Changing what gets published, what is protected,
# or how a vulnerability is reported is a higher-consequence edit than a feature change.
/LICENSE                 @moose
/SECURITY.md             @moose
/CONTRIBUTING.md         @moose
/CODEOWNERS              @moose
/.moosenet-pipeline.yaml @moose
/.gitattributes          @moose

# The specs this repository is built from, and the reviews that shaped them.
/specs/                  @moose
/reviews/                @moose

# Documentation and repo identity.
/README.md               @moose
/behavior-spec.md        @moose
/docs/                   @moose

# Brand assets. Anything shipped to a client bundle gets the same scrutiny as code.
/assets/                 @moose
